WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.860
A bulk RNA reading rises.

2
00:00:03.280 --> 00:00:04.660
Did any cell turn the gene up?

3
00:00:06.160 --> 00:00:09.420
Chapter one ended on a lining whose mix of cells had changed.

4
00:00:10.780 --> 00:00:14.640
Gold clones spread, though on average their cells divided no faster.

5
00:00:15.960 --> 00:00:18.460
Now grind that lining up and measure one RNA.

6
00:00:21.950 --> 00:00:27.110
Suppose, with made-up numbers, each gold cell holds 20 copies of one RNA.

7
00:00:28.590 --> 00:00:29.890
Each blue cell holds 10.

8
00:00:31.190 --> 00:00:34.410
Suppose our bulk assay reports copies per average cell.

9
00:00:35.433 --> 00:00:37.933
Early on, gold cells are 2 in 10.

10
00:00:39.113 --> 00:00:44.073
Two tenths of 20, plus eight tenths of 10, gives 12 copies per average cell.

11
00:00:45.167 --> 00:00:48.947
Later, the clones have spread, and gold cells are 6 in 10.

12
00:00:50.247 --> 00:00:53.207
No single cell changes how much RNA it holds.

13
00:00:54.527 --> 00:00:57.447
Does the bulk reading go up, go down, or stay the same?

14
00:01:03.017 --> 00:01:08.797
It goes up. Six tenths of 20, plus four tenths of 10, gives 16.

15
00:01:10.117 --> 00:01:13.657
The reading rose by a third, yet no cell turned its gene up.

16
00:01:14.917 --> 00:01:15.957
Only the mix changed.

17
00:01:17.000 --> 00:01:18.940
That is the idea of this lesson.

18
00:01:20.200 --> 00:01:25.060
A tissue can change because which cells are there changes, not only because its

19
00:01:25.060 --> 00:01:26.260
cells behave differently.

20
00:01:27.620 --> 00:01:30.480
On its own, one average cannot tell those apart.

21
00:01:31.533 --> 00:01:35.253
To estimate the mix, measure sampled cells one at a time.

22
00:01:36.633 --> 00:01:42.033
Single-cell RNA sequencing usually starts by separating tissue into single cells,

23
00:01:42.533 --> 00:01:43.533
often with enzymes.

24
00:01:45.033 --> 00:01:49.953
Each captured RNA is copied into DNA carrying a short barcode for its cell.

25
00:01:50.983 --> 00:01:54.943
Sequencing produces a count for each gene in each sampled cell.

26
00:01:56.223 --> 00:02:01.063
The result is a table, with one row per gene and one column per sampled cell.

27
00:02:02.723 --> 00:02:06.343
Software groups cells with similar expression profiles into clusters.

28
00:02:07.567 --> 00:02:11.867
Clusters are annotated by comparing their genes with known profiles.

29
00:02:13.167 --> 00:02:14.707
Now the shift can be estimated.

30
00:02:16.007 --> 00:02:21.307
The gold-profile group contains more sampled cells, while its within-group RNA level

31
00:02:21.307 --> 00:02:22.307
stays fixed.

32
00:02:23.250 --> 00:02:25.090
But what does a cluster represent?

33
00:02:26.330 --> 00:02:32.030
It might mark a cell type, a relatively stable identity such as a keratinocyte or T

34
00:02:32.030 --> 00:02:37.810
cell. It might instead mark a state within one type, such as dividing or

35
00:02:37.810 --> 00:02:42.110
differentiating. This distinction depends on the question.

36
00:02:43.233 --> 00:02:45.153
Or it might reflect the method.

37
00:02:46.433 --> 00:02:49.233
Breaking up tissue can stress cells before capture.

38
00:02:50.793 --> 00:02:53.973
Cells from samples processed separately can split by batch.

39
00:02:55.193 --> 00:02:58.573
A clustering resolution setting can split or merge groups.

40
00:02:59.617 --> 00:03:03.897
A cluster is an observation; what it represents is a hypothesis.

41
00:03:05.397 --> 00:03:08.497
A snapshot cannot reveal ancestry or future fate.

42
00:03:09.877 --> 00:03:11.317
Lineage tracing tests those.

43
00:03:12.797 --> 00:03:16.157
Perturbation tests cause, and protein can check an annotation.

44
00:03:17.367 --> 00:03:21.527
Back in the lining, gold cells became common, so the average moved.

45
00:03:22.787 --> 00:03:24.087
No cell made more RNA.

46
00:03:25.407 --> 00:03:28.887
Change the mix; the average moves without within-cell change.
